  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|6x-2|}{8x+5}=$",
  "worked_solution": "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|6x-2|=6x-2.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|6x-2|}{8x+5}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x-2}{8x+5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x-2}{8x+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6x}{x}-\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{8x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6-\\frac{2}{x})}{(8+\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-0}{8-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
